CAT = "auwhiteEmerg"; SEG = "EMG"
Q = [
("Which of these must be notified to the WHS regulator?",
 "A worker's serious head injury", ["A minor cut treated on site", "A bruised knee from a slip", "A splinter removed by first aid"],
 "Notifiable incidents are a death, a serious injury or illness, or a dangerous incident. A serious head injury needing immediate treatment is one; minor first aid injuries are not."),
("How soon must a notifiable incident be reported to the regulator?",
 "Immediately after learning of it", ["Within 7 days of it happening", "Within 30 days of it happening", "At the next toolbox meeting"],
 "The PCBU must notify the regulator immediately after becoming aware of a notifiable incident, by the fastest means available."),
("If the regulator asks for written notice of a notifiable incident, it must be given within:",
 "48 hours", ["24 hours", "7 days", "14 days"],
 "Under the model WHS Act, written notice must be given within 48 hours of the regulator's request."),
("After a notifiable incident, the site must be left undisturbed except to:",
 "Help the injured or make it safe", ["Clean up ready for the next shift", "Restart work on schedule", "Remove damaged plant"],
 "The site must be preserved until an inspector arrives or directs otherwise. Exceptions include helping an injured person, removing a deceased person, making the site safe and police requirements."),
("Which of these is a dangerous incident that must be notified even if nobody is hurt?",
 "Collapse of a trench wall", ["A flat tyre on a site ute", "A minor diesel drip", "A broken office window"],
 "Dangerous incidents include the collapse of an excavation or structure, uncontrolled escapes of gas or chemicals, electric shocks and falls of objects from height. They are notifiable without any injury."),
("Why should a near miss be reported?",
 "So it is fixed before injury", ["To find someone to blame", "Only so insurance claims can be paid", "It doesn't need reporting"],
 "A near miss shows a hazard that could hurt someone next time. Reporting it lets the cause be fixed so it does not happen again."),
("Who should you first report a hazard to on site?",
 "Your supervisor", ["The police", "The local council", "The client"],
 "Report hazards to your supervisor straight away, and to your HSR if one is elected. Follow the site's hazard reporting procedure."),
("How long must a PCBU keep records of notifiable incidents?",
 "At least 5 years", ["At least 1 year", "At least 2 years", "At least 10 years"],
 "Under the model WHS Act, records of each notifiable incident must be kept for at least 5 years from the day the regulator was notified."),
("What number do you call for fire, police or ambulance in Australia?",
 "000", ["911", "999", "111"],
 "Triple zero (000) is Australia's emergency number. 911 is used in North America, 999 in the UK and 111 in New Zealand."),
("When the evacuation alarm sounds on site, you should:",
 "Go to the assembly point", ["Finish your task first", "Collect all your tools", "Wait in the lunch room"],
 "Stop work, make your equipment safe if it takes only a moment, and go to the assembly point by the safest route so everyone can be counted."),
("After an evacuation, when can you go back into the work area?",
 "When the warden gives the all clear", ["As soon as the alarm stops sounding", "After waiting 10 minutes", "When your own crew decides"],
 "Only the emergency warden or emergency services can declare it safe to return. An alarm stopping does not mean the danger has passed."),
("Why should you never use a water extinguisher on an electrical fire?",
 "Water conducts electricity", ["Water spreads too slowly", "It will freeze the cables", "It cannot reach high fires"],
 "Water conducts electricity, so spraying it on live equipment can electrocute the user. Use carbon dioxide or dry chemical powder instead."),
("Which extinguisher is suited to a fire in live electrical equipment?",
 "Carbon dioxide (CO2)", ["Air-pressurised water", "Wet chemical", "Foam"],
 "Carbon dioxide does not conduct electricity and leaves no residue, so it suits electrical fires. Dry chemical powder also works."),
("In Australia, a red fire extinguisher with a black band contains:",
 "Carbon dioxide", ["Water", "Foam", "Dry chemical powder"],
 "Australian extinguishers are red with a coloured band: black for carbon dioxide, white for dry chemical powder, blue for foam and oatmeal for wet chemical. A plain red one holds water."),
("A red extinguisher with a blue band contains foam. It is mainly for:",
 "Flammable liquid fires", ["Live electrical fires", "Cooking oil fires", "Burning metal fires"],
 "Foam smothers flammable liquid fires and also works on ordinary combustibles. It is not for live electrical equipment."),
("A white band on a red fire extinguisher means it contains:",
 "Dry chemical powder", ["Carbon dioxide", "Foam", "Wet chemical solution"],
 "A white band marks dry chemical powder, which works on ordinary combustibles, flammable liquids and electrical fires, depending on the type."),
("A fire blanket is best used for:",
 "A small fat or clothing fire", ["A large building fire", "A fire in a roof space", "A fast-spreading grass fire nearby"],
 "A fire blanket smothers a small fire, such as burning cooking oil or a person's clothing, by cutting off the oxygen."),
("In the PASS method for using an extinguisher, what does the 'A' stand for?",
 "Aim at the base of the fire", ["Aim at the top of the flames", "Alert your supervisor first", "Activate the nearest alarm"],
 "PASS is Pull the pin, Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle and Sweep side to side. Aiming at the flames misses the fuel."),
("A fire hose reel is suited to fires in:",
 "Wood, paper and fabric", ["Live electrical gear", "Cooking oil and fat", "Flammable liquids"],
 "Hose reels deliver water, which suits ordinary combustibles such as wood, paper and fabric. Water on electrical, fat or liquid fires can make things worse."),
("Where should you learn who the site's first aid officer is and where the kit is kept?",
 "At the site induction", ["From a chemical's SDS", "From the scaffold tag", "From the regulator"],
 "The site-specific induction covers first aid arrangements, emergency procedures, assembly points and amenities for that site."),
("In the first aid action plan DRSABCD, what does the first D stand for?",
 "Check for danger", ["Defibrillate now", "Dial 000 at once", "Do chest compressions"],
 "DRSABCD is Danger, Response, Send for help, Airway, Breathing, CPR, Defibrillation. Check for danger first so you do not become a second casualty."),
("A chemical spills on site. What should you do first?",
 "Clear the area and raise the alarm", ["Hose it straight into the stormwater drain", "Mop it up without any gloves", "Cover it with sawdust and leave"],
 "Keep people away, raise the alarm and follow the spill procedure in the SDS and the site's emergency plan. Washing chemicals into drains causes pollution."),
("You smell a strong gas leak near hot work. What should you do?",
 "Stop work, evacuate and report", ["Finish the weld, then report", "Turn on fans and keep working", "Find the leak with a lighter"],
 "Gas near hot work can explode. Stop the work, move people away, raise the alarm and follow the emergency procedure."),
("A worker gets a small cut that is treated with first aid. What else is needed?",
 "Report and record it", ["Nothing, it was minor", "Notify the regulator now", "Call an ambulance"],
 "Every injury should be reported and recorded under the site's procedure so causes can be fixed. A minor first aid injury is not a notifiable incident."),
]
